        s4: '4. Network address',
        s4Intro: (p) =>
          `<p>The \\( 32 \\) bits of the IP are written and, exactly below them, those of the mask. The <b class="ipn-red-strong">red</b> rectangle gathers both rows in the columns where the mask is \\( 1 \\): its length, \\( ${p} \\) bits, is set by the \\( 1 \\)s of the mask, but the bits to keep are those <b>of the IP</b> lying inside the rectangle. These bits are copied and the \\( 32 \\) bits are completed by writing \\( 0 \\) in all the remaining columns: the result is the <b>network address</b>. The IP bits outside the rectangle are crossed out with a grey X, because they are not carried over.</p>`,
        s4IntroNone:
          '<p>The mask contains no \\( 1 \\), so no column is selected: no bit of the IP is kept and the network address consists of \\( 32 \\) zeros.</p>',
        s4And:
          '<p>This operation is the <b>bitwise AND</b> of IP and mask: in each column the result is \\( 1 \\) only if both the IP bit and the mask bit are \\( 1 \\).</p>',
        backIntro: (what) =>
          `<p>Each octet ${what} is now converted back to decimal by positional expansion: each bit is multiplied by the power of \\( 2 \\) corresponding to its position, counted from the right starting at \\( 0 \\), and the terms are added.</p>`,
        backNet: 'of the network address',
        backHost: 'of the host part',
        octCopied: (k, v) =>
          `<p><b>Octet ${k}</b>: all its \\( 8 \\) bits are copied from the IP, so it is identical to octet ${k} of the IP and its value is \\( ${v} \\).</p>`,
        octZero: (k) => `<p><b>Octet ${k}</b>: it contains only added zeros, so its value is \\( 0 \\).</p>`,
        octExpand: (k, bits) =>
          `<p><b>Octet ${k}</b>: \\( \\text{${bits}}_{2} \\) contains both bits copied from the IP and added zeros; its value is computed:</p>`,
        netResult: '<p>The network address is therefore:</p>',
        binWord: 'binary',
        decWord: 'decimal',

        s5: '5. Host part',
        s5Intro: (h) =>
          `<p>The same two rows are drawn again. Now the <b class="ipn-green-strong">green</b> rectangle gathers only the columns where the mask is \\( 0 \\): it is \\( ${h} \\) bits long. The IP bits lying inside the rectangle are copied and \\( 0 \\) is written in all the network columns, on the left, until the \\( 32 \\) bits are complete: the result is the <b>host part</b>. The IP bits outside the rectangle are crossed out with a grey X, because they are not carried over.</p>`,
        s5IntroNone:
          '<p>The mask contains no \\( 0 \\), so there are no host bits: the host part consists of \\( 32 \\) zeros.</p>',
        s5And:
          '<p>This operation is the <b>bitwise AND</b> of the IP and the <b>negated mask</b>, that is the mask with NOT applied, which turns every \\( 0 \\) into \\( 1 \\) and every \\( 1 \\) into \\( 0 \\).</p>',
        hostResult: '<p>The host part is therefore:</p>',

        s6: '6. Summary',
        sumRows: ['IP address (decimal)', 'IP address (binary)',
          'Subnet mask (decimal)', 'Subnet mask (binary)',
          'CIDR prefix', 'Network bits', 'Host bits',
          'Network address (decimal)', 'Network address (binary)',
          'Host part (decimal)', 'Host part (binary)'],
        checkText:
